-- Three posts from the October 2026 keyword research (DataForSEO, US, English).
-- Keyword data and the reasoning behind each topic: docs/seo/keyword-research-2026-10.md.
-- Re-runnable: each insert skips a slug that already exists.

-- ============================================================================
-- 1. local seo for small business (1,300/mo, KD 21)
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.ryzon_landing_blog (slug, title, excerpt, content, meta_title, meta_description, focus_keyword, secondary_keywords, category, tags, internal_links, cta_text, cta_href, is_published, published_at)
values (
'local-seo-for-small-business',
'Local SEO for Small Business: How to Get Your Business on Google',
'A plain-English, step-by-step guide to showing up when people nearby search for what you do: your Google Business Profile, reviews, your website and the listings that matter.',
$c$<section><h2 id="what-is-local-seo">What local SEO actually means</h2><p>When someone searches "plumber near me" or "hair salon in Troy", Google shows two kinds of results: a map with three businesses on it (the "map pack"), and a list of websites underneath. <strong>Local SEO is everything you do so your business appears in both places</strong> for searches in your area.</p><p>The good news for a small business: you are not competing with every company in the country. You are competing with the handful of businesses near your customers. Most of what moves the needle is free, and you can do it in a few evenings.</p><p>Google says local results are based on three things:</p><ul><li><strong>Relevance:</strong> how well your listing and website match what the person searched for.</li><li><strong>Distance:</strong> how close you are to the searcher, or to the place they named.</li><li><strong>Prominence:</strong> how well known and trusted your business looks online, including reviews and mentions on other sites.</li></ul><p>You can't move your shop closer to every customer, but you control almost everything about relevance and prominence. The steps below are in the order we'd do them.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="google-business-profile">Step 1: Claim and complete your Google Business Profile</h2><p>Your Google Business Profile (it used to be called Google My Business) is the listing that shows in the map pack and on the right side of search results. It is free, and it is the single biggest lever in local SEO.</p><h3 id="claim-and-verify">Claim and verify it</h3><p>Search for your business name on Google. If a listing already exists, choose "Own this business?" to claim it. If not, create one at business.google.com. Google will ask you to verify that you're the owner, usually by a video of your location, a phone call, a text or a postcard. Until you're verified, most of your edits won't show.</p><h3 id="fill-in-everything">Fill in every field</h3><ul><li><strong>Business name:</strong> your real name, exactly as it appears on your sign. Adding keywords ("Joe's Plumbing Best Plumber Detroit") breaks Google's rules and can get the listing suspended.</li><li><strong>Primary category:</strong> the most specific one that fits, such as "Plumber" rather than "Contractor". Add secondary categories for other services you really offer.</li><li><strong>Address or service area:</strong> if customers come to you, show your address. If you go to them, hide the address and list the cities or ZIP codes you serve.</li><li><strong>Hours:</strong> including holiday hours. Wrong hours are one of the fastest ways to lose a customer's trust.</li><li><strong>Phone and website:</strong> a local number and a link to your website.</li><li><strong>Services and description:</strong> list each service in plain words. Write the description the way you'd explain your business to a neighbor.</li><li><strong>Photos:</strong> your storefront, your team, your work and your vehicles. Real photos beat stock images every time.</li></ul><h3 id="keep-it-active">Keep it active</h3><p>A profile that gets attention looks like a business that's open and busy. Add new photos every month or two, post updates and offers, and answer the questions people ask on your listing.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="get-more-google-reviews">Step 2: Get more Google reviews (the right way)</h2><p>Reviews help in two ways: they're part of how Google judges prominence, and they're the first thing a customer reads before calling you.</p><ul><li><strong>Ask every happy customer.</strong> The best moment is right after the job is done and they've told you they're pleased.</li><li><strong>Make it one tap.</strong> In your Business Profile, find "Ask for reviews" to get a short link. Text it, put it on your invoice, add a QR code to your counter or business card.</li><li><strong>Reply to every review,</strong> good and bad. Thank people by name. For a bad review, stay calm, apologize for the experience and offer to make it right offline. Future customers read your replies.</li><li><strong>Never buy reviews or offer rewards for them.</strong> Google's policies forbid fake and incentivized reviews, and it removes them, sometimes along with the listing.</li></ul><p>Steady beats sudden. A few new reviews every month looks more natural, and more trustworthy, than fifty in one week followed by silence.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="nap-consistency">Step 3: Make your name, address and phone match everywhere</h2><p>SEO people call this <strong>NAP consistency</strong>: your business Name, Address and Phone number should be written the same way everywhere they appear online. When your details disagree across sites (an old address here, a different phone number there), Google is less sure which information is right, and customers end up calling disconnected numbers.</p><p>Pick one exact format, for example "Suite 200" rather than sometimes "Ste. 200", and use it on your website, your Business Profile and every listing below.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="local-citations">Step 4: List your business on the sites that matter</h2><p>A <strong>citation</strong> is any mention of your business details on another website. You don't need hundreds. Start with the ones customers actually use:</p><ul><li>Bing Places for Business (it also feeds results in other tools)</li><li>Apple Business Connect (for Apple Maps and Siri)</li><li>Facebook, Yelp and Nextdoor</li><li>The Better Business Bureau and your local chamber of commerce</li><li>Directories for your trade or industry, such as a state association or a trade group you belong to</li></ul><p>Once those are done and consistent, extra citations matter much less than reviews and a good website.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="get-your-website-on-google">Step 5: Get your website ready for local searches</h2><p>Your Business Profile gets you into the map. Your website is what convinces Google, and the customer, that you're the right choice. Here's what a local website needs:</p><h3 id="say-what-and-where">Say what you do and where, in plain words</h3><p>Put your main service and your area in the page title and the main headline: "Emergency Plumbing in Royal Oak and Ferndale" says far more to Google than "Welcome to Our Website". Use the words your customers use, not industry jargon.</p><h3 id="one-page-per-service">Give each main service its own page or section</h3><p>A page about water heater installation can rank for water heater searches. A single page that lists twelve services in one sentence usually ranks for none of them.</p><h3 id="show-your-service-area">Show your service area</h3><p>List the towns and neighborhoods you serve, and put your full address and phone number in the footer of every page, matching your Business Profile exactly.</p><h3 id="phone-and-speed">Make it fast and easy on a phone</h3><p>Most local searches happen on phones. Your number should dial with one tap, your pages should load quickly on mobile data, and the main button should be easy to find with a thumb.</p><h3 id="local-business-schema">Add local business structured data</h3><p>Structured data (schema markup) is hidden code that tells search engines your business name, address, hours, phone and services in a format they can read with certainty. Most website builders don't add it for you. A professionally built site should.</p><h3 id="search-console">Tell Google your site exists</h3><p>Set up Google Search Console for free, verify your website, and submit your sitemap (usually yoursite.com/sitemap.xml). That's how you get your website on Google faster, and Search Console then shows you which searches bring people to your site. Bing Webmaster Tools does the same for Bing and can import your setup from Google.</p><div class="callout"><p>Not sure your site has these basics? Our <a href="/blog/local-service-business-website-checklist">10-point local business website checklist</a> walks through them one by one.</p></div></section>
<section><h2 id="ai-search">What about AI search?</h2><p>Google now shows AI-written summaries above many results, and more people ask tools like ChatGPT for recommendations. These tools pull their answers from the same places: business profiles, reviews and websites that state clearly what a business does, where it works and what it costs.</p><p>So local SEO isn't dead. The basics in this guide are exactly what AI answers rely on. A clear website with your services, area, prices and real reviews gives them something accurate to repeat.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="local-seo-checklist">Your 30-day local SEO checklist</h2><ol><li><strong>Week 1:</strong> claim, verify and fully complete your Google Business Profile, with photos.</li><li><strong>Week 2:</strong> write down your exact name, address and phone format, then fix your website, Facebook, Yelp, Bing Places and Apple Business Connect to match.</li><li><strong>Week 3:</strong> get your review link, add it to invoices and texts, and ask your last ten happy customers.</li><li><strong>Week 4:</strong> check your website against Step 5, set up Google Search Console and submit your sitemap.</li><li><strong>Every month after:</strong> add photos, reply to every review and keep your hours current.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3 id="faq-local-seo-cost">How much does local SEO cost for a small business?</h3><p>The basics in this guide cost nothing but your time: a Google Business Profile, review requests, listings and Search Console are all free. Agencies charge monthly fees for ongoing local SEO work. Before paying for that, make sure the free foundations are done, because no agency can make up for a missing profile or a website that doesn't say where you work.</p><h3 id="faq-worth-it">Is local SEO worth it?</h3><p>For a business that serves a local area, yes. People searching "near me" or "in [your town]" are usually ready to call or visit. Showing up in front of them, with good reviews, is one of the lowest-cost ways to win customers.</p><h3 id="faq-how-long">How long does local SEO take to work?</h3><p>Changes to your Business Profile can show up within days. Rankings from reviews, listings and website improvements build over weeks and months. The businesses that win are the ones that keep at it steadily.</p><h3 id="faq-without-website">Can I rank on Google Maps without a website?</h3><p>You can appear in the map with just a Business Profile, but a website helps you rank and helps customers choose you. Google uses your website to understand what you do, and customers use it to check your prices, photos and reviews before they call.</p><h3 id="faq-seo-dead">Is SEO dead now with AI?</h3><p>No. AI summaries and chat tools need trustworthy sources, and for local businesses those sources are your profile, your reviews and your website. Clear, accurate information helps you show up in both regular results and AI answers.</p></section>$c$,
'Local SEO for Small Business: Step-by-Step',
'A plain-English local SEO guide for small businesses: set up your Google Business Profile, get reviews, fix your listings and get your website on Google.',
'local seo for small business',
array['how to get my business on google','how to get my website on google','google business profile optimization','how to get more google reviews','how to rank higher on google maps','nap consistency','local citations','local seo checklist'],
'how-to',
array['local seo','google business profile','getting found'],
'[{"href":"/blog/local-service-business-website-checklist","text":"The 10-point local business website checklist"},{"href":"/blog/contractor-website-design","text":"Contractor website design that wins jobs"},{"href":"/#pricing","text":"Plans with SEO built in"},{"href":"/#how-it-works","text":"How the free demo works"}]'::jsonb,
'Get My Free Demo', '/#demo', true, now()
) on conflict (slug) do nothing;

-- ============================================================================
-- 2. contractor website design (1,000/mo, KD 12)
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.ryzon_landing_blog (slug, title, excerpt, content, meta_title, meta_description, focus_keyword, secondary_keywords, category, tags, internal_links, cta_text, cta_href, is_published, published_at)
values (
'contractor-website-design',
'Contractor Website Design: What Plumbers, HVAC Pros, Roofers and Electricians Need to Win More Jobs',
'What a contractor website needs to turn searches into booked jobs: the pages, the trust signals, trade-specific must-haves and the SEO basics, with no fluff.',
$c$<section><h2 id="why-it-matters">Your website is your best salesperson</h2><p>When a pipe bursts or the AC quits in July, people grab their phone, search, and call one of the first businesses that looks trustworthy. Your website has a few seconds to answer three questions: <strong>Do you do this job? Do you work in my area? Can I trust you?</strong> If the answer isn't obvious, they hit back and call the next contractor.</p><p>Good contractor website design isn't about fancy animations. It's about making those three answers impossible to miss, and making it effortless to call or request a quote.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="above-the-fold">What visitors must see without scrolling</h2><p>The top of your homepage, the part people see before they scroll, does most of the work. It needs:</p><ul><li><strong>A headline with your trade and area:</strong> "Roof Repair and Replacement in Oakland County" beats "Quality You Can Trust".</li><li><strong>A tap-to-call phone number</strong> in the header, big enough to hit with a thumb.</li><li><strong>One main button:</strong> "Get a Free Quote" or "Book Service". One clear action, repeated down the page.</li><li><strong>A trust line:</strong> years in business, licensed and insured, your star rating and review count.</li><li><strong>A real photo</strong> of your crew, your truck or your work.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="pages-you-need">The pages every contractor website needs</h2><h3 id="home-page">Home page</h3><p>Your headline, main services, service area, reviews and a clear call to action. Think of it as the summary that points people to the right next page.</p><h3 id="service-pages">A page for each main service</h3><p>"Water heater installation", "drain cleaning" and "sewer line repair" should each have their own page. Each page explains what the job involves, roughly what affects the price, how quickly you can come out, and ends with a call to action. Separate pages are also how you show up for each of those searches on Google.</p><h3 id="service-area-pages">Service area</h3><p>List the towns and neighborhoods you cover. If you serve several distinct cities, a short page for each one, with real details about the work you do there, helps you rank in each place. Avoid copying the same page and swapping the town name; Google treats that as low quality.</p><h3 id="projects-page">Projects or gallery</h3><p>Before-and-after photos are the most persuasive content a contractor can have. Add a sentence or two per project: what the problem was, what you did, and where.</p><h3 id="about-page">About</h3><p>Who you are, how long you've been doing this, your licenses and certifications, and photos of the team. People are letting you into their homes; a face and a name matter.</p><h3 id="reviews-page">Reviews</h3><p>Show real reviews with first names and towns, and link to your Google reviews so people can see they're genuine.</p><h3 id="contact-page">Contact and quote request</h3><p>Phone, email, hours, service area and a short form. Ask only for what you need to call them back: name, phone, address or ZIP, and a quick description of the job. Every extra field costs you leads.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="trust-signals">Trust signals that win the call</h2><ul><li><strong>License and insurance:</strong> state it clearly, with license numbers. Check your state's licensing board, since some states require your license number on all advertising, including your website.</li><li><strong>Reviews and ratings:</strong> your Google rating near the top, with a few recent reviews further down.</li><li><strong>Guarantees and warranties:</strong> spell out what you stand behind.</li><li><strong>Memberships and certifications:</strong> manufacturer certifications, trade associations, the BBB.</li><li><strong>Real photos:</strong> stock photos of smiling models in spotless hard hats make people trust you less, not more.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="by-trade">What each trade needs on its website</h2><h3 id="plumber-websites">Websites for plumbers</h3><p>Lead with emergency service if you offer it: "24/7 emergency plumber" near the top, with the phone number right beside it. Give drain cleaning, water heaters, leak repair and sewer lines their own pages, and say how fast you can usually get there.</p><h3 id="hvac-websites">HVAC websites</h3><p>Heating and cooling are seasonal, so your homepage should change with the weather: AC repair and tune-ups in spring and summer, furnace repair in fall and winter. Feature maintenance plans and financing prominently, because they're what turns one-time calls into repeat customers.</p><h3 id="roofing-websites">Roofing websites</h3><p>Roofing is a big, considered purchase, so photos and proof carry the most weight. Show before-and-after projects, the materials and brands you install, your warranty, and how you handle storm damage and insurance claims. A "free roof inspection" offer is a natural main button.</p><h3 id="electrician-websites">Electrician websites</h3><p>Safety and licensing come first; make your license impossible to miss. Give your most-requested jobs their own pages, such as panel upgrades, EV charger installation, generator hookups and lighting, and say clearly whether you handle residential, commercial or both.</p><h3 id="landscaping-websites">Landscaping and lawn care websites</h3><p>Landscaping sells with photos. Lead with your best before-and-after work, make seasonal services easy to find (spring cleanups, weekly mowing, snow removal), and offer a simple quote form that asks for the address and property size.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="contractor-seo">SEO for contractors: getting found, not just looking good</h2><p>A beautiful website nobody finds won't ring your phone. The basics of contractor SEO:</p><ul><li><strong>Use the words customers search:</strong> "roof repair" and "emergency plumber", in your page titles and headlines, together with your town names.</li><li><strong>One page per service, so each can rank on its own.</strong></li><li><strong>A complete Google Business Profile</strong> with the right category, your service area, photos and a steady flow of reviews.</li><li><strong>Fast, mobile-friendly pages,</strong> because most searches happen on phones.</li><li><strong>Local business structured data,</strong> so search engines read your name, area, hours and phone with certainty.</li></ul><p>Our full guide to <a href="/blog/local-seo-for-small-business">local SEO for small businesses</a> walks through each step.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="mistakes">Common contractor website mistakes</h2><ul><li>A phone number that's tiny, buried in the footer, or not tappable on a phone.</li><li>No prices at all. Even "most water heater installs run between X and Y" helps people decide to call.</li><li>One "Services" page that lists everything in a single paragraph.</li><li>Photos that are clearly stock, or no photos at all.</li><li>A homepage slideshow that hides the headline.</li><li>Contact forms with ten fields.</li><li>A site that hasn't been updated in years, with old hours, old services or a broken form.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="what-it-costs">What a contractor website costs</h2><p>Prices range from a do-it-yourself builder subscription to thousands of dollars for a custom agency build, plus hosting and upkeep. We break down every option in <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost">how much a small business website costs</a> and the ongoing side in <a href="/blog/website-maintenance-cost">website maintenance costs</a>.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>How we do it:</strong> we design a free demo of your new site first, built around your trade and your area. You see it before you pay anything. Plans start at $40/month with a $0 design fee. See our <a href="/#examples">example designs</a>, including plumbing, landscaping and cleaning businesses.</p></div></section>
<section><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3 id="faq-how-much">How much does it cost to hire someone to design a contractor website?</h3><p>It depends on the route. A do-it-yourself builder is the cheapest in money but costs you evenings. Freelancers and agencies usually charge a one-time design fee plus hosting and updates. Monthly plans spread the cost out with no big bill up front. Our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost">website cost guide</a> compares all three.</p><h3 id="faq-how-to-build">How do I build a contractor website?</h3><p>Start with your list of services and the towns you serve, gather real photos and a few reviews, then build the pages above: home, one page per service, service area, projects, about, reviews and contact. Whether you use a builder or hire someone, make sure the phone number, main button and trust signals are on every page.</p><h3 id="faq-three-seconds">What is the 3-second rule in website design?</h3><p>It's a rule of thumb that visitors decide almost immediately whether to stay. For a contractor, that means your trade, your area and your phone number should be clear the moment the page loads, without scrolling.</p><h3 id="faq-diy">Should I use Wix or Squarespace for my contracting business?</h3><p>Builders can work if you have the time to set them up properly: writing every service page, adding structured data, compressing photos and keeping it updated. Many contractors would rather spend those hours on jobs and have someone else handle the website.</p><h3 id="faq-how-many-pages">How many pages should a contractor website have?</h3><p>At least a home page, a page for each main service, a contact page and an about page. Add a projects gallery, a reviews page and service area pages as you grow. Quality matters more than count: five genuinely useful pages beat twenty thin ones.</p></section>$c$,
'Contractor Website Design That Wins More Jobs',
'Contractor website design that turns searches into booked jobs: the pages, trust signals and SEO plumbers, HVAC pros, roofers and electricians need.',
'contractor website design',
array['website for contractors','websites for plumbers','hvac website design','roofing website design','electrician website','best contractor websites','contractor seo','roofing seo'],
'guides',
array['contractors','home services','web design'],
'[{"href":"/#examples","text":"See example designs for local businesses"},{"href":"/blog/local-seo-for-small-business","text":"Local SEO for small business"},{"href":"/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost","text":"How much a small business website costs"},{"href":"/#pricing","text":"Plans and prices"}]'::jsonb,
'Get My Free Demo', '/#demo', true, now() - interval '1 hour'
) on conflict (slug) do nothing;

