-- 3. website maintenance cost (480/mo, KD 5) + website maintenance services (1,600, KD 5)
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.ryzon_landing_blog (slug, title, excerpt, content, meta_title, meta_description, focus_keyword, secondary_keywords, category, tags, internal_links, cta_text, cta_href, is_published, published_at)
values (
'website-maintenance-cost',
'Website Maintenance Cost for Small Businesses: What You Should Really Pay',
'Domain, hosting, security, updates and help when something breaks: what website maintenance actually includes, what each part costs, and how to avoid overpaying.',
$c$<section><h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2><p>Search for website maintenance costs and you'll find ranges like "$5 to $25,000 a month". That's technically true and completely useless if you run a salon, a café or a plumbing business.</p><p>For a typical small business website, with a handful of pages, a contact form and no online store, maintenance comes down to a few predictable pieces: a domain name, hosting, security, small updates and someone to call when something breaks. Below is what each one is, what it usually costs, and when you're paying too much.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="what-maintenance-includes">What website maintenance actually includes</h2><ul><li><strong>Domain name:</strong> your address, like yourbusiness.com, renewed every year.</li><li><strong>Hosting:</strong> the server that keeps your site online and fast.</li><li><strong>Security certificate (SSL):</strong> the padlock in the browser bar. Browsers warn visitors away from sites without one.</li><li><strong>Software updates and security:</strong> keeping the platform, themes and plugins patched so the site isn't hacked.</li><li><strong>Backups:</strong> copies of your site you can restore if something goes wrong.</li><li><strong>Content updates:</strong> new hours, prices, photos, services, staff.</li><li><strong>Fixes:</strong> broken forms, broken layouts on new phones, links that go nowhere.</li><li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> someone noticing when the site goes down, before your customers do.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="cost-breakdown">Cost breakdown, piece by piece</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical cost</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Domain name</td><td>About $10 to $25 a year for a .com</td><td>Watch for cheap first-year prices that renew much higher.</td></tr><tr><td>Hosting</td><td>A few dollars to about $30 a month for basic hosting; more for managed hosting</td><td>Cheap shared hosting can be slow. Speed affects both customers and Google.</td></tr><tr><td>SSL certificate</td><td>Often free</td><td>Most good hosts include one. Paying extra is rarely necessary for a small business.</td></tr><tr><td>Updates, security and backups</td><td>Your time, or part of a care plan</td><td>Builders like Wix and Squarespace handle the software; WordPress sites need regular updates.</td></tr><tr><td>Content changes and fixes</td><td>Hourly or included in a plan</td><td>Published guides from hosting companies put freelance rates at roughly $25 to $150 an hour.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Add those up and a simple small business site can cost very little to keep online. The real cost driver is <strong>who does the work</strong> and how often you need changes.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="four-ways-to-pay">The four ways small businesses pay for maintenance</h2><h3 id="do-it-yourself">1. Do it yourself</h3><p>You pay for the domain and hosting or a builder subscription, and do everything else yourself. It's the cheapest in money and the most expensive in time. It works if you enjoy it and have the evenings; it fails quietly when updates slip and the site drifts out of date.</p><h3 id="freelancer-hourly">2. A freelancer, by the hour</h3><p>You pay when you need something done. That's fine for occasional changes, but costs are unpredictable and response times depend on their schedule. Make sure someone is actually keeping the software updated, not just making changes when you ask.</p><h3 id="agency-care-plan">3. An agency care plan</h3><p>A monthly retainer covering updates, backups, monitoring and a set amount of changes. Predictable, but check exactly how many changes are included and what counts as "extra".</p><h3 id="all-in-one-plan">4. An all-in-one monthly website plan</h3><p>Some companies, including us, combine the design, the website and its ongoing care in one monthly price, often with no design fee up front. The appeal is simplicity: one bill, one person to call. The trade-off is an ongoing payment, so read what happens if you ever leave.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="do-you-need-monthly-maintenance">Do websites need monthly maintenance?</h2><p>Every website needs some upkeep, but how much depends on how it's built:</p><ul><li><strong>WordPress and other self-hosted platforms</strong> need regular software updates. An outdated plugin is one of the most common ways small business sites get hacked.</li><li><strong>Website builders</strong> handle software updates for you, but you still need to keep the content current.</li><li><strong>Modern, professionally hosted static sites</strong> have far fewer moving parts to patch, so upkeep is mostly content changes.</li></ul><p>For every type, the content side doesn't go away. Old hours, old prices and dead links cost you customers, and Google notices sites that are never updated.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="overpaying">Signs you're overpaying</h2><ul><li>You pay a monthly maintenance fee but can't name what was done last month.</li><li>Every small change, such as new hours or a new photo, is billed as a separate project.</li><li>You're paying for premium hosting or a paid security certificate on a five-page website.</li><li>Your domain renews at a price several times higher than standard registrars charge.</li><li>You're paying for "SEO maintenance" with no reports and no changes you can see.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="red-flags">Red flags to check before you sign</h2><ul><li><strong>Know who owns what.</strong> Ask who the domain is registered to and what happens to your website files if you leave. A good provider answers in writing.</li><li><strong>Know what's included.</strong> How many changes per month? How fast do they respond? Are backups and updates included or extra?</li><li><strong>Know how to leave.</strong> What does cancelling cost, and what do you get to keep?</li></ul></section>
<section><h2 id="redesign-or-maintain">When to redesign instead of maintaining</h2><p>Sometimes paying to patch an old site is throwing good money after bad. It's usually time for a new website when it looks broken or tiny on phones, is slow to load, can't be edited without a developer, no longer matches your services, or simply looks dated next to your competitors. At that point a redesign often costs less over a year than repeated fixes.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="how-we-price">How we handle it</h2><div class="callout"><p><strong>Ryzon Automation plans</strong> start at $40/month with a $0 design fee. On the Starter and Growth plans, simple tasks may be free depending on how long you've been with us, and other changes are quoted at $20/hour. The Complete plan includes free changes and additions for the life of your site. We manage the site for the first year; after that, if you decide to leave, we export your site files and hand them over. See all the details on our <a href="/#pricing">pricing</a>.</p></div><p>Still comparing build costs too? Read <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-small-business-website-cost">how much a small business website costs</a>.</p></section>
<section><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3 id="faq-pay-someone">How much should I pay someone to maintain my website?</h3><p>For a simple small business site, you're mostly paying for someone's time: keeping software updated and making changes. Hourly freelance rates vary widely, so compare what's included rather than the headline price. A plan that includes a set amount of changes is usually easier to budget for than open-ended hourly billing.</p><h3 id="faq-monthly">Do websites need monthly maintenance?</h3><p>They need regular attention. Self-hosted platforms like WordPress need frequent software updates for security. Every site needs its content kept current. Whether that's a monthly plan or occasional work depends on how your site is built and how often your business changes.</p><h3 id="faq-hosting-cost">How much does website hosting cost for a small business?</h3><p>Basic hosting ranges from a few dollars to around $30 a month, and managed hosting costs more. A small business site doesn't need expensive hosting, but it does need hosting that's fast, secure and backed up.</p><h3 id="faq-domain-cost">How much does a domain name cost?</h3><p>A .com domain typically costs around $10 to $25 a year. Watch the renewal price, not just the first-year deal, and make sure you know who the domain is registered to.</p><h3 id="faq-costly">Is it costly to maintain a website?</h3><p>It doesn't have to be. For most small businesses, the domain and hosting are modest; the bigger cost is the time to keep it updated. That's where a clear, all-in-one plan or a reliable freelancer earns its keep.</p></section>$c$,
